<!--Post: 11099--><!--Time: 1110428387-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11099"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Hbtcontrol<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Hbtcontrol<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, March 09, 2005 - 8:19 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->According to BTK&#39;s eighth grade reunion profile, he said he was stationed in Okinawa, Greece and someplace that he used the abbreviation of ALB for.  Wasn&#39;t there an air force training base in Albuerque New Mexico at that time?  Isn&#39;t that also where the Otero children went back to live? <BR> <BR>Not that it means much now that he&#39;s caught - but is it possible that BTK did meet the Otero&#39;s in Albuerque? I know Otero was stationed in Panama but maybe they had lived in Albuerque and were home for a visit?  I always kind of wondered how the Otero&#39;s ended up in the small town of Wichita Kansas.  Maybe from a conversation Joe had with BTK at one time?   <BR> <BR>Still speculating....I need to get a life again.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11099-->
<!--Post: 11124--><!--Time: 1110445184-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11124"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Bestbud<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bestbud<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, March 10, 2005 - 12:59 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Kirtland Air Force Base - Albuquerque, NM <BR> <BR>ALB is also an Air Force Term for Air Land Battle.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11124-->
<!--Post: 11148--><!--Time: 1110477804-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11148"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:linnieskid@earthlink.net" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Linnieskid<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Linnieskid<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, March 10, 2005 - 10:03 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->John Eric Armstrong is a serial killer who was in the Navy and killed about 6 women while stationed in various places overseas.  This was in addition to the murders in the U.S. I&#39;d bet my bottom dollar that Dennis has left a trail of bodies wherever he was stationed<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11148-->
<!--Post: 11157--><!--Time: 1110485603-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11157"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Nightdriver<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Nightdriver<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, March 10, 2005 - 12:13 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I thought it read Korea, Greece, Turkey, and Okinawa. Someone here posted the dates as Aug. 1966 to Aug. 1969, not for sure abuot those dates. Yates was stationed in Germany from 1980 to 1988. There were supposed to have been 20 murders of German females around the base at that time. US Army did not allow German police to question any US citizens on the base.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11157-->
<!--Post: 11171--><!--Time: 1110488963-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11171"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:embersageblue@aol.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Embersage<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Embersage<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, March 10, 2005 - 1:09 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Here is the information I have, and we are currently working on the Air Force years, One of the girls on the board&#39;s family is all military so she is looking thru records on unsolved murders during that time.  <BR>August 17, 1966 Rader joined The USAF <BR>Served four years with extented stint in South Pacific <BR>Stationed in Kadean AFB Okinawa Japan and Tachikawa Air Base in Japan <BR>Left active duty on August 9, 1970 <BR>Reserve duty until December 1972 <BR>Earned small arms expert marksman badge AF <BR>Speciality: Outside cable and Antenna Technician <BR> <BR> <BR>That is all we have so far. Now keep in mind when I was in the reserves we did AT, and traveled all over US and Hawaii<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11171-->
<!--Post: 11209--><!--Time: 1110503450-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11209"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Hbtcontrol<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Hbtcontrol<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, March 10, 2005 - 5:10 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->If you look at the form he filled out for his 8th grade reunion, <a href="http://www.kansas.com/multimedia/kansas/archive/btk/022705raderriverview.pdf" target=_top>http://www.kansas.com/multimedia/kansas/archive/bt k/022705raderriverview.pdf</a> <BR> <BR>it says Air Force - ALB, Turkey, Greece, Okinawa, Japan&#41; and then underneath what looks like &#40;Korea&#41; 1966-1970. <BR> <BR>I believe that Albuquerque was a training base at that time &#40;and abbreviated as ALB&#41; and probably his first stop in the Air Force. <BR> <BR>B. <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11209-->
<!--Post: 11211--><!--Time: 1110504633-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11211"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:embersageblue@aol.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Embersage<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Embersage<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, March 10, 2005 - 5:30 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->thank you for that Information it will help a lot<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11211-->
<!--Post: 11246--><!--Time: 1110527349-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11246"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Nightdriver<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Nightdriver<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, March 10, 2005 - 11:49 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Not quite. In 1966, Kirtland was operated by the Air Force Special Weapons Command. It was responsible for testing and evaluation of nuclear weapons. The training mission was much earlier. Also, If he was at Kadena, guess what was there at that time? Yep, Blackbirds. It would be nice to know the units he was assigned to, then you could trace which bases he was at.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11246-->
<!--Post: 11253--><!--Time: 1110530332-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11253"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, March 11, 2005 - 12:38 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->From this the &#34;From&#34; BTK board here, posted on Dec. 9th, 2004, by a poster calling themselves ChristOntheCross: <BR> <BR><i>&#34;...My duty in the military consisted of <b>radio work which kept my</b> &#40;sic&#41;<i> in the field</i> and allowed for several opportunities to kill others...&#34;</i>  <BR> <BR>From an <a href="http://www.kake.com/home/headlines/1355157.html">article posted at KAKE.com on March 10, 2005, titled &#34;Former Friend Speaks&#34;</a>: <BR> <BR><i>&#34;...It was 1966; the country was at war in Vietnam. Gerry </i>&#40;Tremmel Jr., the &#34;friend&#34;&#41;<i> says he and Rader joined the Air Force at the same time and spent a year and a half rooming together, traveling and horsing around. They built <b>communications antennas and towers for the military</b>, training in Wichita Falls, Tex. then shipped off to Greece and Turkey. <BR> <BR>&#39;He was kind of a quiet guy,&#39; Gerry says. &#39;He sort of stayed by himself, didn’t get into a lot of trouble.&#39; <BR> <BR>He says the last time he spoke to Rader was in July 1968, when their squadron closed and they went their separate ways. Rader signed a picture for Gerry, saying, &#39;Let’s go get some of that Alabama sun.&#39;...&#34;</i> <BR> <BR>The questions the above info raises are many.  <BR> <BR>- Was the ALB Rader put on that 8th grade reunion information sheet Alabama? What did he do once in the Alabama sun? <BR> <BR>- If the poster &#34;ChristOnTheCross&#34; was BTK - and we know he was using his church&#39;s computer in early December, so the deep, ugly irony in the screen name is hard to ignore - then he was telling us flat-out that he&#39;d killed before anyone knew what was happening. Think about just how anonymous he was, travelling with the AF through all those places. Putting up transmission towers and antennae for radio broadcast is a journeyman sort of job, and does definitely keep a guy &#34;in the field&#34;, in both the military and civilian life. <BR> <BR>And training in Wichita Falls. I felt a tiny bit creeped by that, as it was a guess I&#39;d made when the 11/30/04 info was released, and it appeared BTK had referred to going to a &#34;tech school&#34; while in the military. My own father trained at that school at the Sheppard AF Base, so it was a logical guess to me. <BR> <BR>One must ask about the unsolveds from the 60&#39;s in Wichita Falls, if any, and perhaps northern Alabama. The base he was likely to have been stationed at there would be, I think, in Huntsville.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11253-->
<!--Post: 11261--><!--Time: 1110545420-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11261"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Itty<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Itty<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, March 11, 2005 - 4:50 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I believe the article states he trained at Wich. falls tx, then shipped out.  How can we find out how long training would last?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11261-->
<!--Post: 11288--><!--Time: 1110557318-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11288"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, March 11, 2005 - 8:08 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Itty, I quoted that part. <i>&#34;They built communications antennas and towers for the military, training in Wichita Falls, Tex. then shipped off to Greece and Turkey.&#34;</i> <BR> <BR>Depending on the training, my father - who attended that school between &#39;70-&#39;71 thinks it could be as long as a year, but the normal amount of time was 6 months, which is how long we were there. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
